  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Vanuatu, the PPIU implemented a software upgrade to Vanuatu Abattoir that automated production identification, traceability, and carcass categorisation to improve efficiency and accuracy with stock inventory and yield calculations. These functions help Vanuatu improve and expand market access options by meeting importing country requirements in Australia, New Zealand, Japan, and China. The PPIU also invested in equipment needed for sanitary and phytosanitary (SPS) testing for Geolab, Vanuatu. Equipping GeoLab with the tools needed to conduct SPS testing enhanced Vanuatu&#x2019;s in&#x2013;country capacities, directly contributing to reduced reliance on overseas laboratories and improved trade readiness of Vanuatu&#x2019;s private sector.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Trade</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">PACER Plus&#x2019; implementation continues to deliver tangible outcomes for Parties. The ASYCUDA World customs platform has now been deployed for all Pacific Parties&#x2019; Customs Administration systems. The Australia and New Zealand backed PACER Plus Implementation Unit (PPIU) provided targeted technical assistance and capacity building, in collaboration with UN Trade and Development, to facilitate system implementation and enhance customs officials&apos; capability and effectiveness. This has reduced goods clearance times from&#x202f;11 days to two days in Tuvalu, and from three days to three hours in Kiribati. PACER Plus also enabled government and industry alignment to international product standards addressing key Non-Tariff Barriers. Samoa, Cook Islands and Vanuatu received installation and maintenance support for incinerators to meet each other&#x2019;s biosecurity requirements. PACER Plus piloted a new intra-Pacific Labour Mobility arrangement between Niue and Solomon Islands by facilitating an Inter-Agency Understanding between the Solomon Islands Labour Mobility Unit and the Niue National Labour Mobility Steering Committee. Four Solomon Island nurses were able to work in the Niue health sector for six months from August 2024.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
